{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1117506-religion-and-glamour","id":1117506,"title":"Religion and Glamour","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Changeling: The Dreaming"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-07-17T17:05:56-07:00","id":1117506,"title":"Religion and Glamour","content":"Recently, I had a rather long and curious discussion with one of my players. Long story short, we were talking and wondering about how to handle Glamour and organized religion in our Changeling game. Now I'm in a bit of a impasse and I could really need some new perspectives:<br /> The issue is this: Is organized religion inspiring or banality inducing? <br /> On the one hand, organized religion are dogmatic, full of inflexible and unmoving dogma and usually reject frivolity, excess or stigmatize acts of whim and inspiration.<br /> But on the other hand, some of the most beautiful works of art and artistry were inspired by it. Church altars and stain glass windows tell stories, mosques are marvels of architecture and I could go on. So... how do you people handle Kith who find god and get into these kinds of things?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13654-davesknd","name":"Davesknd","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"173"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-17T17:12:25-07:00","id":1117510,"title":"","content":"It can be either, like most things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10243-zenten","name":"zenten","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10243_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"488"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-17T17:51:42-07:00","id":1117517,"title":"","content":"You touch upon the answer I think. The great religious teachers generally break long held standards. Faith-filled rebels against banal dogma..","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#181818\"><span style=\"font-family:Merriweather\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">\u201cHumpty had always sat on walls, it was his way.\u201d </span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#181818\"><span style=\"font-family:Merriweather\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">\u2015 </span></span></span><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Lato\"><b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Jasper Fforde, </span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#181818\"><span style=\"font-family:Merriweather\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/work/quotes/2504943\" target=\"_blank\">The Big Over Easy</a></span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1756-mythadvocate","name":"MythAdvocate","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1756_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"444"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-17T18:54:52-07:00","id":1117535,"title":"","content":"Okay, I'm going to speak as a member of a minority among gamers. I'm a born-again, evangelical Christian. I came to that point when I was 28 and spent a good bit of my life up to that point as an agnostic. I only mention that as a preface to my comments so you can see where I'm coming from. I'm not going to try and convert anyone and will be respectful of anyone's beliefs.<br /> <br /> My belief in God and my decision to follow Christ fundamentally changed who I am. Once I made that decision I started down the road of being nicer and more caring. Before that I was very moody and was deliberately manipulative with people I knew. My whole outlook on life changed, and it led me to being a much better person. In a way, I went through a chrysalis when I was &quot;saved&quot;, turning into a new being (as the Bible says will happen). Since that point nearly 20 years ago I have really delved into Bible study, learned hymns and worship choruses, and looked at the world around me with new eyes. I'm a scientist and suddenly I started seeing true wonder in God's world. Molecular biology amazed me with God's design. Astronomy astonished me with it's incredible balance leading to our very special planet. Each time I look at any aspect of science I see the beauty of God's creation. I've felt His presence in my life, seen it change me and people around me, and felt my heart lifted through prayer and song.<br /> <br /> Yes, religion can be extremely Glamour-filled! A true belief in a higher being can be incredibly inspiring and uplifting! Some of the most amazing art ever created had religious themes and were made as a way to honor God. Principles such as genetic inheritance and the Big Bang theory were developed by theologians. Many hospitals, charitable organizations, and even universities were started by religious groups. Amazing lyrics and poems have been written by people expressing their love for God.<br /> <br /> Now the flip-side. As a Christian I am absolutely annoyed and dismayed by the frequent closed-mindedness of may church-goers. I've seen so many people hurt by organized religion and pushed away from God by so-called &quot;loving&quot; Christians (Westboro Baptist, anyone?). Look at how religious extremists in many Muslim countries have instituted policies and laws oppressing women and brutalizing homosexuals. Spend enough time behind the scenes in a Christian church and you'll likely come across the people who are gossips and hateful, interested only in the specific tradition of their church and showing none of Jesus's teachings. People in any religion can become trapped in specific rituals and traditions, going through the motions because that's what they are supposed to do, not because they really believe in it. I've seen more people that I can count in churches and religious groups whom I can only describe as Autumn People.<br /> <br /> A true belief in God can be full of Glamour. Organized religion can contribute to that (look at how much wonderful art the Catholic Church has produced). But religious organizations can also be some of the most banal groups around and can suck Glamour right out of a person, stifling their creativity in the name of &quot;god&quot;. <br /> <br /> In my games I've never shied away from religion, but I've never pushed it on my players. I've had NPCs with devout faith that were still able to function as well as any other changeling. If you look at House Liam you will see that they had strong ties to religion, especially Christianity, and were condemned for protecting churches. I think that in a C:tD game you can use religion as a horrible thing or a wonderful thing. It depends on the themes of your story.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://imgbb.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://image.ibb.co/fzeOgQ/1172c70f3ff188688852c74c8145285c_original_2_4.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> <br /> Visit my freehold for Changeling links, settings, art, and other goodies.<br /> <a href=\"https://theshepherdsfreehold.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">The Shepherd's Freehold</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13648-shepherd","name":"Shepherd","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13648_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"84"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T05:56:21-07:00","id":1117689,"title":"","content":"I dont think faith, by itself, could produce Glamour, because Glamour happens when you produce something, when you actually create something Glamour creative and beautiful (even in a sad way) that ties to the Dreaming of the Dreamer. Faith based works of course can, all those Cathedrals, paints, mosaics, and the Whole freaking Vatican, can produce Glamour, but it is not due to their religion imagery or the religion behind it. Is just that religion can be a very powerfull (and rich) fomenter of artistic productions.<br /> <br /> But, as pointed by <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13648-shepherd\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar13648_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"13648\" data-vbnamecard=\"13648\">Shepherd</a>, faith can make you less banal and more easily able to see the wonder of the world in a religious point of view. I think it is a minority of people who actually get less banal with religion, as most religions and churches I have seen here are quite cynical and more focused on prayers and requests (and payments) than in doing God's good work and actually helping people. Also, being good and nice to people also has nothing to do with being banal or glamorous.<br /> <br /> So, as a rule of thumb, I would ignore what the church says and the religious aspect of it and focus more on what it does and produces. Does it has a Choir? I might be a glamorous thing, if people there is doing for the music, or for the faith, and if they create musics, not only repeat it. If people are singing there just because they need the money (not quite sure if Churches' Choirs pay people, but follow my thought) or because they are demanded by the family as a tradition or something. It can be inspiring, and even good to hear, but nonetheless banal. Even the impact of entering a church, full of followers praying in unison, with the light entering through the stained glass might be Glamour inducing, but I dont think it would generate Glamour beyond the first times people get inside of it.<br /> <br /> If you are asking about a Changeling going to church, sure, why not? Maybe avoiding the more banal ones, for obvious reasons, and too much preaching about &quot;the Demon inside you&quot; and &quot;their powers to alter perception&quot; might create a religious fanatic Dauntain, but that is the exception instead of the rule. Mostly will just go to church, do their religious thing, and go on with their lives.<br /> <br /> // Just saying, I refer to church in a general way. It doesn't focus on any particular religion or faith. The example are more related to the Christian cases just because that is the one I'm more familiar with. */<br /> <br /> But Shepherd make me though about one thing: could a scientist or researcher generate Glamour by finding a formula or way to solve a hard scientific problem, like a solving a complicated matter on a specific topic after months of research or explaining the way a certain thing in a topic of study work? In a historical example, Einstein could have been a Dreamer on the field of physics?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12934,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12934-manbat","user":"Manbat","date":". 07-18-2017, 08:59 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12934-manbat","name":"Manbat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12934_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T06:49:00-07:00","id":1117703,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Manbat</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I dont think faith, by itself, could produce Glamour, because Glamour happens when you produce something, when you actually create something Glamour creative and beautiful (even in a sad way) that ties to the Dreaming of the Dreamer. Faith based works of course can, all those Cathedrals, paints, mosaics, and the Whole freaking Vatican, can produce Glamour, but it is not due to their religion imagery or the religion behind it. Is just that religion can be a very powerfull (and rich) fomenter of artistic productions.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not a Changeling expert, but I thought Glamour wasn't only creativity. Love it's another fount of glamour, isn't it?. Aren't there other Glamour inducing acts that don't involve &quot;a product&quot; in the material sense?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T07:02:22-07:00","id":1117707,"title":"","content":"Yeah, you don't just have to make art to produce Glamour. Anything listed under the Musing Thresholds certainly counts, for instance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10243-zenten","name":"zenten","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10243_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"488"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T07:06:09-07:00","id":1117709,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1117506-religion-and-glamour?p=1117703#post1117703\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not a Changeling expert, but I thought Glamour wasn't only creativity. Love it's another fount of glamour, isn't it?. Aren't there other Glamour inducing acts that don't involve &quot;a product&quot; in the material sense?</div> </div> </div> </div>Seconded. Its not so much about creativity, and produced art, its more about whether it inspires creativity. If it takes you out of the hum-drum of life and brings you into deeper realms, its Glamour. If it crushes your hopes and reams and keeps you from inspiriation, its banal.<br /> <br /> And of course scientists can be Dreamers. Exploring mysteries is what its all about, and the out of the box thinkers always make the big discoveries. But as in all fields, there are always those without vision controlling the money. And such creatures are generally very banal.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#181818\"><span style=\"font-family:Merriweather\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">\u201cHumpty had always sat on walls, it was his way.\u201d </span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#181818\"><span style=\"font-family:Merriweather\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">\u2015 </span></span></span><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Lato\"><b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Jasper Fforde, </span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#181818\"><span style=\"font-family:Merriweather\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/work/quotes/2504943\" target=\"_blank\">The Big Over Easy</a></span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":1756,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1756-mythadvocate","user":"MythAdvocate","date":". 07-18-2017, 10:57 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1756-mythadvocate","name":"MythAdvocate","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1756_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"444"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T09:56:36-07:00","id":1117816,"title":"","content":"Any feeling or inspiration can be Glamour inducing, in the sense of making a Dreamer produce a Glamour filled object, or just reduce the general banality of a person, but in the sense of Reverie, aka producing Glamour, it need some sort of work in real life. Not necessarily tangible, but perceptible and recordable (like a song, poem, sculpture or dance). In the musing threshold is Create Love, but making a person fall in love for another might reduce her banality, in specific, but would not induce more Glamour in the world unless the Dreamer put his hands to work and make a song, a poem, a nice dating request, or anything real.<br /> My point was the simple feeling of faith does not induce Glamour, nor it usually reduce peoples banality. At least as far as I have seem, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13648-shepherd\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar13648_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"13648\" data-vbnamecard=\"13648\">Shepherd</a> is an exception, mostly people I have seem that are very religious are not deeply Glamourous.I saw some that became less banal after a faith change, 'cause they saw the world different and everything, but that is usually just for a while.<br /> <br /> Talking about musing thresholds, what happened with Sad Glamour? Like, the glamour produce by sad poems, hopeless tales and the songs of unrequited love? Is it Ravaging? Cause Ravaging mention nothing about the need to produce something, as Reverie do. Is it just a side effect of Ravaging? Aren't sad and tragic stories still Glamorous, linked to the Dreams in his own, dark way?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12934-manbat","name":"Manbat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12934_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T10:07:59-07:00","id":1117818,"title":"","content":"Light and darkness have nothing to do with it. You could Muse a serial killer, and get them to stop (at least awhile) through Ravaging.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10243-zenten","name":"zenten","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10243_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"488"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T10:27:23-07:00","id":1117828,"title":"","content":"I agree with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10243-zenten\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar10243_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"10243\" data-vbnamecard=\"10243\">zenten</a>. I'm sure that Diego de Deza was a man overflowing with Glamour. <br /> Anyways, thank you guys a lot for the input. That really helped a lot","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13654-davesknd","name":"Davesknd","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"173"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T10:50:49-07:00","id":1117842,"title":"","content":"I agree with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10243-zenten\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar10243_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"10243\" data-vbnamecard=\"10243\">zenten</a> too, but the Musing Thresholds dont leave a lot of space to inspire negative feelings. I mean, which Musing Threshold would be better to inspire a Goth Dreamer to write sad poems? Create Calm, Create Hope, Foster Dreams and Help those in Need seems counterproductive to make he feel the depths of sadness ans sorrows he need to produce his Art. Inspire Creativity with a sad loner is even harder. Foster Trust that other people feel the same sadness as him? Create Love so he can feel unrequited? What would you do?<br /> Am I just not getting it? Is this the wrong thread to be asking it?<br /> <br /> By the way, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13654-davesknd\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"13654\" data-vbnamecard=\"13654\">Davesknd</a>, glad it helped. And who you think helped Diego de Deza to create such ingenious torture devices? Nothing like a nice Unsellie Nocker for your most Nefarious Needs. )","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12934-manbat","name":"Manbat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12934_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T11:05:25-07:00","id":1117853,"title":"","content":"A lot of that sounds like the sad goth poets I know. And van Gogh and Picasso were not certainly not very happy people.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10243-zenten","name":"zenten","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10243_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"488"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T11:20:17-07:00","id":1117863,"title":"","content":"A few Ideas:<br /> <br /> Create an impossible love, a romantic's inspiration if there has ever been one.<br /> <br /> Foster Trust may be necessary for a shy goth to realize that other people would empatize with her sad feelings if she were to write them.<br /> <br /> Foster Dreams that are so utopic and detached from reality as to make reality seem a dirty dark place (sadness usually comes from loss).<br /> <br /> Create Calm so the artist can concentrate in it's work (being alone in a dark night with no other worry than to reflect on your life can be surprisingly depressing, and, hey, you know what you can do?, write those feelings).<br /> <br /> Inspire Creativity: Just do it. Show a lot of sad things, impart a lot of sad experiences, be sure that Joy it's nowhere arround without Ravaging the goth. It's there a limit of what you can do to &quot;Inspire Creativity&quot;?, because it seems fairly generic in a world where Glamour it's gained trough a creation(If every muse inspires a creation, then all muses are &quot;inspiring creativity&quot;). As far as I know you could ruin christmass and say it was for the sake of &quot;inspiration&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 07-18-2017, 02:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T11:46:34-07:00","id":1117872,"title":"","content":"I think that the <i>very short </i>list of Muses, taken as a strict rule like a Discipline, limits the game.<br /> <br /> If Inspire Love works, why not other powerful and important feelings?. A lot of tales and myths are about revenge and sorrow, why not inspire righteous fury?, why not inspire faith?, why not inspire negative emotions?.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T12:05:26-07:00","id":1117875,"title":"","content":"I take the list as the most common Thresholds among the Seelie.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10243-zenten","name":"zenten","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10243_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"488"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T13:01:57-07:00","id":1117895,"title":"","content":"The way it was written seem to be that those are the only thresholds there are. But I agree, there is no reason to be, but them it make it look much more ravaging, that even take the same time, has thresholds that are like Create Anger.<br /> I think I would differentiate them by making the Ravaging something more fast, in order to actually take away the Dreamer Creativity, to push him, like punishing him for his Dreams and his arts, taking up the Glamour from the person. Reverie is about inducing feeling, to help him produce something, to create something, positive or negative. If you need to trash the Christmas Party to do it, go on. Ravaging is not about creating, or helping to create, is about punishing the Dreamer for having Dreams, about destroying Dreams and eating the broken pieces.<br /> Is not as bad as Rhapsody, cause it destroy the connection to the dreaming of the dreamer.<br /> The Reverie is helping the tree to give Fruits, and everybody can enjoy it. Everyone, even mortals, get at least some inspiration from it.<br /> The Ravaging is cutting the biggest branchs before it give Glamour Fruits and eating them. Is better than getting nothing, but take time to make another one.<br /> The Rhapsody is forcing shoving fruits under the roots to boost the tree to give fruits when it should not, taking all the fruits and making a juice, and hoping that the tree can give fruits once again in the future.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12934-manbat","name":"Manbat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12934_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"112"}]},"thankedb